# ── Secrets / credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
# Any .env variant (.env.local, .env.vm, …) — but keep the committed template.
.env.*
!.env.example
# Keys, certs, and service-account JSON, wherever they land. why they stay even
# though no such file exists here and nothing in the repo writes one: this block is
# prophylactic, not a record. `service-account*.json` is the one with a real
# consumer (score_jobs.py authenticates to Vertex via GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS);
# the rest are the shapes a credential arrives in when someone drops it beside the
# code, and the cost of an unmatched pattern is a leaked key that cannot be unleaked.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
service-account*.json
credentials.json
secrets.json
*.secrets.json
# Backups written next to atomically-saved configs (.env.bak holds secrets;
# config.json.bak etc. mirror git-ignored configs); never commit them.
*.bak

# ── Personal / user-supplied content (commit the *.example templates instead) ─
resume.md
resume_tailor_files/master_experience.yaml
# Covers the user's resume_sample.pdf (style example the engine reads via
# config.EXAMPLE_PDF) and any personal resume PDFs kept beside the master.
resume_tailor_files/resume_*.pdf
# The curated style exemplar (config.STYLE_EXEMPLAR_TXT): the user's own résumé bullets,
# one per line, so it is personal content exactly like the PDF it takes priority over.
resume_tailor_files/style_exemplar.txt
local/config.json
# Sidecar lock (locks.file_lock) and the PID-tagged temp an atomic write leaves
# behind if the replace fails — both land next to the config they guard.
*.json.lock
*.json.*.tmp
search_config.json
scoring_config.json
# Legacy flat answer file: apply_answers.py migrates a pre-existing one on first
# run, so a user upgrading from an older version can still have it here.
apply_config.json
apply_answers.json

# ── LaTeX build artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# why these are here when the tailored PDFs are not: compile.py runs pdflatex with
# cwd set to the .tex's own folder, and that folder is RESUME_TAILOR_OUTPUT, which
# defaults to ~/Downloads/Generated_Resumes, outside the tree. These patterns cover
# the user who repoints that env var at a path inside the repo.
*.aux
# why so broad: `*.log` also covers the pipeline's runtime logs (scraper.log,
# watcher.log, open_dashboard crash log) — no log file in this project is committed.
*.log
*.synctex.gz
*.out

# ── Generated / runtime data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# why *.db rather than the old `seen.db`: the live application tracker has never
# lived in the tree (seen_db.py has resolved it under %LOCALAPPDATA% since the
# first commit), so that one name could never match. What CAN land here is a
# Tracker -> Export backup of the same personal history: main_window.py offers a
# save dialog defaulting to inployed-tracker-<date>.db, and the user can point it
# anywhere. The extension is the thing worth covering. No tracked file is a .db.
*.db
# *.csv covers the scraped job data (linkedin_jobs_master.csv, run_stats.csv, the
# per-run-label outputs); the json/txt below are scraper/scorer run-state.
*.csv
*.csv.gz
last_run_job_ids.json
external_exclude_ids.json
score_state.json
company_blocklist.txt
# local scrape rows queued for the VM (deleted after confirmed push)
outbox/
# local pipeline output dirs: per-run-label scrape/score results and
# manually-added jobs (real scraped data — the *.csv rules above already
# cover their contents; this keeps stray non-CSV run files out too)
morning/
afternoon/
evening/
night/
manual/

# ── Python ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
# The optional project-local venv README Step 2 creates and the .cmd launcher
# prefers (venv\Scripts\pythonw.exe). `.venv/` is not a name this repo uses
# anywhere; it stays because uv and `python -m venv .venv` produce it, and a
# committed virtualenv is the single worst thing that can land in a clone.
venv/
.venv/
# Packaging artifacts. pyproject.toml holds tool config only (no [build-system],
# nothing to build), so these should never appear -- ignored so one can't slip in.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# ── OS / editor / IDE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# why these stay when none has ever been committed here: they are per-contributor
# junk rather than project files, so today's absence says nothing about the next
# clone. .DS_Store is kept even though macOS sits outside the support matrix,
# because osopen.py carries a darwin branch and the tree gets read on Macs.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Settings snapshots written on every Save. Each snapshot copies the live .env
# (which holds secrets, per the user's "self-contained snapshots" choice), so the
# whole archive must never be committed.
local/settings_archive/

# linked git worktrees (never committed)
.worktrees/

# offscreen UI screenshot output (scripts/ui_screenshots.py)
.screenshots/

# walkthrough video (scripts/build_walkthrough.py). Rebuilt on demand and served
# from GitHub's user-content CDN, which is the only place an inline player works,
# so the repo has no reason to carry the binary.
docs/walkthrough.mp4
